# Modern LLMs
@slug: modern-llms
@description: A practical map from neural-network foundations to useful language models.
@order: 1

@section before-you-start | Before you start
## This is not a calculus course.

The goal of this course is to build an approximately comprehensive understanding of modern large language models (LLM), not go through a first year undergraduate math sequence.

We will use concepts from calculus, linear algebra, statistics and information theory as assumed knowledge. More in-depth material, like stochastic differential equations, manifolds, or specific derivations will be explained, though not from first principles.

::component Callout
{
  "title": "The prerequisite",
  "body": "You should be familiar with stochastic gradient descent, and some undergraduate mathematics; specifically up to differential equations, distributions, and linear transformations."
}
::end

@section recommended-foundations | Recommended foundations
## Start here if backpropagation still feels fuzzy.

I recommend these videos from Grant / 3Blue1Brown. They were there for my initiation on the subject, and they still hold up timelessly for a visual, intuitive introduction to neural networks, gradient descent, and backpropagation. The fourth video finally connects the idea to calculus.
::component YouTubePlaylist
